000003_000467_000505_4f02242557382a4b","GTC_000003_000735_000803_08eb0fa088914078","GTC_000003_000888_000926_1105a256f237de67","GTC_000003_001276_001326_fbce12643ee2ccfa"],"family_id":"FGF_8ccb9c8959a9923177e93830","family_id_provenance":{"algorithm":"sha256","canonical_input":{"artifact_ids":["GTC_000003_000467_000505_4f02242557382a4b","GTC_000003_000735_000803_08eb0fa088914078","GTC_000003_000888_000926_1105a256f237de67","GTC_000003_001276_001326_fbce12643ee2ccfa"],"case_id":"CASE_72448D73D60F"},"canonicalization":"json-sort-keys-compact-utf8","case_id_included":true,"digest_hex":"8ccb9c8959a9923177e93830d71b611303648b73c534e2918318384e1ac3dae2","digest_prefix_length":24},"family_label":"Git commands for recording and publishing a file rename","rationale":"The first generated technical content appears in turn_000003, directly triggered by the file-rename question in turn_000002. All four same-response shell candidates form a coherent Git rename workflow covering rename, commit, push, and rename-similarity handling.","response_event_index":3,"response_turn_id":"turn_000003","status":"complete","target_prompt_event_index":2,"target_prompt_id":"turn_000002"}},"first_generation_response":{"create_time":1700100793.571477,"event_index":3,"role":"assistant","source_node_id":"a15495a0-6bd8-4eb5-8a32-bf31b003b5da","source_parent_id":"aaa29fa8-b681-46da-82ea-801fd443c969","source_record_index":3,"text":"To have GitHub recognize a file rename in a Pull Request (PR) and display it correctly in the diff viewer, you need to ensure that the rename operation is understood by Git as a move, rather than a delete and add operation. Here's how you can achieve this:\n\n1. **Use Git Rename Command:**\n   - If you haven't committed the changes yet, you can use the Git command to rename the file. This is typically done using the `git mv` command. Here's an example:\n     ```bash\n     git mv old_filename new_filename\n     ```\n   - This command tells Git that you're renaming a file, which helps it track the file as a move instead of a delete/add operation.\n\n2. **Commit and Push:**\n   - After renaming the file, commit your changes:\n     ```bash\n     git commit -m \"Renamed file from old_filename to new_filename\"\n     ```\n   - Then push the commit to the branch that's part of the PR:\n     ```bash\n     git push origin your-branch-name\n     ```\n\n3. **Similarity Threshold:**\n   - Sometimes, if you've made significant changes to the file content along with the rename, Git might not recognize it as a rename. In such cases, you can adjust the similarity threshold. A lower threshold might help Git to detect it as a rename.\n   - You can set this threshold when committing:\n     ```bash\n     git commit -m \"Your commit message\" -- -M50%\n     ```\n     Here, `50%` is the similarity index. You can adjust this percentage based on your needs.\n\n4. **Check the Pull Request:**\n   - After pushing your changes, check the PR on GitHub. The file should now be shown as renamed rather than deleted and added.\n\n5. **If Changes Are Already Committed:**\n   - If you have already committed the delete and add, you might need to adjust the commit history. This can be complex and may involve commands like `git rebase` or `git reset`. It's important to be cautious with these commands, especially if you're working on a shared branch.\n\nRemember, for GitHub to recognize a file as renamed, the contents of the file should also be somewhat similar. If the file is completely different, Git might still consider it as a delete and add, even if you use `git mv`.","turn_id":"turn_000003"},"target_developer_prompt":{"create_time":1700100764.435001,"event_index":2,"role":"user","source_node_id":"aaa29fa8-b681-46da-82ea-801fd443c969","source_parent_id":"d2e3da39-5e76-4de9-b533-88bf61aa0a28","source_record_index":2,"text":"in a github PR, I have renamed a file. in the diff viewer, it's showing one file deleted and another added, but i instead want to show the file as re